signed graphs are equivalent if they can be obtained from each other by a series of switchings. Jin, Kang, Steffen [7] proved the following lemma which plays an important role in investigating coloring of signed graphs.\n\nLemma 1.1\n\nLet (G,\\sigma ) be a signed graph, and {\\sigma }^{\\prime } be obtained from \\sigma by a switch at X. If (G,\\sigma ) is k-colorable, then (G,{\\sigma }^{\\prime }) is also k-colorable.\n\nLemma 1.1 implies that the equivalent signed graphs have the same chromatic number. Let (G,\\sigma ) be a signed graph and H be a subgraph of G. For convenience, throughout this paper, we use (H,\\sigma ) instead of (H,\\sigma {\\mid }_{H}) where is the restriction of \\sigma to H.\n\nSteinberg in [10] conjectured that every planar graph without 4-cycle and 5-cycle is 3-colorable. Erdös suggested the following problem: does there exist a constant k such that a planar graph without cycles of length from 4 to k is 3-colorable? Abbott and Zhou [1] proved that such a k exists and . This result was later improved to by Borodin in [3] and, independently, Sanders and Zhao [9] and to by Borodin et al. [4]. Recently, Cohen-Addad, Hebdige, Král, Li and Salgado [5] showed that Steinberg’s conjecture is false. This means that the signed graph version of Steinberg’s conjecture is also false. On the other hand, Erdös’ problem is still open for the original version. One naturally ask what the signed graph version of Erdös’ problem is. Motivated by the above observations, we present the following theorem.\n\nTheorem 1.2\n\nLet (G,\\sigma ) be a signed planar graph without cycles of length from 4 to 8. If f is a face of degree at most 12 in G, then every 3-coloring of (G\\left[V\\left(f\\right)\\right],\\sigma ) can be extended to a 3-coloring of (G,\\sigma ).\n\nA classical theorem of Grötzsch [6] says that every triangle-free planar graph G is 3-colorable. So far, we do not know whether the signed graph version of Grötzsch’s theorem is true. However, Máčajavá, 